#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-onboarding-derivation -- the operator onboarding guide published on
# kiosk.tech teaches a worked verb whose ORIGINAL lives in this repository, and
# until this script nothing compared the two (K-1324, K-1325, K-1326;
# extended to the WRITE verbs by K-1338 and K-1339).
#
# THE DEFECT, measured rather than argued. `kiosk.tech/onboarding.html` is the
# page an operator follows from `bundle install` to a served wire. It carries a
# full controller pair as copyable Ruby, and every line of it is a copy of
# something here: the class names are the ones the `kiosk:install` generator
# writes into an initializer as its worked example, and the domain -- catalogue,
# delivery windows, settlement-gated reschedule -- is kiosk-demo-getgrocery's.
#
# On 2026-09-06 three drifts were found there by a human reading the page:
#
#   * getgrocery's `delivery_slots` had made `date` OPTIONAL and
#     `delivery_address` REQUIRED, for a reason its own code states -- the
#     caller cannot compute the operator's today. The page still taught
#     `required: ["date"]`, called `params.require(:date)`, had no
#     `delivery_address` at all, and froze a calendar literal into
#     `example_params` -- a date already in the past, which the live verb
#     refuses. The drift landed in `ede32f70` and was found TEN HOURS later.
#   * The page taught `params[:delivery_slot_id].to_i`, which turns `"1.5"`
#     into slot 1 inside the declared 1..6 range. Both demo origins refuse it,
#     and one of them names the idiom outright.
#   * The page said getgrocery's handlers were the classes it shows. They are
#     not: getgrocery registers `Kiosk::StorefrontController`, the page shows
#     `Kiosk::CatalogController`, and `git grep -n "CatalogController"` over
#     this repository answers with the e2e fixture, this gem's README, the
#     install-generator template and specs -- none of them getgrocery.
#
# Two more were found one verb over on 2026-09-06, and they are why the rules
# below are no longer scoped to a single named verb:
#
#   * The page REQUIRED `delivery_date` on both write verbs. getgrocery
#     requires it on neither -- omitting it books tomorrow, for the same reason
#     `delivery_slots` leaves its own `date` optional: the caller cannot compute
#     the operator's today. So the page taught a stricter contract than the
#     origin it was copied from (K-1338).
#   * The page's `create_order` indexed every cart entry without checking it is
#     a Hash, so `items: ["bread"]` -- a well-formed request with the wrong
#     shape -- raised inside the handler and reached the caller as a 500 about
#     the operator's server, where getgrocery's own guard answers a typed 400
#     naming the shape it wanted (K-1339).
#
# The first of those also found a bug in this script rather than only on the
# page: `required_list` returned the FIRST `required:` in a verb's region, which
# for `create_order` is the ITEM schema's `required: ["sku", "qty"]`, nested two
# braces down. Both sides answered with that nested list, so the comparison was
# two copies of one irrelevant fact and passed on a page that required an
# argument getgrocery leaves optional. `top_level_required` walks brace depth
# instead; the plant that exposed it is a self-test arm.
#
# NOTHING HELD ANY OF IT, IN EITHER REPOSITORY. `bin/check-demo-derivations`
# compares each demo's `before-after.md` against THAT demo's own producers and
# its file set is this repository; `kiosk.tech/bin/check-derived-counts` holds
# typed cardinals, not code blocks, and its own header records why it did not
# reach across the boundary for one. So the guide could drift from its source
# indefinitely, and it did -- onto a published page.
#
# ── WHY IT LIVES HERE, AND WHAT THAT COSTS ──────────────────────────────────
#
# The comparison needs a file from each repository, and neither CI has the
# other checked out. MEASURED at head 2026-09-06 with `command grep -c
# "repository:"`: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` here -> 0, and the five workflow
# files in the sibling -> 0 between them. So a checkout-based reach would run
# on a developer's machine and silently no-op in every CI job, which is the
# "reads as coverage, holds nothing" shape this workspace keeps re-discovering.
# `check-derived-counts` weighed exactly this and declined it for a cardinal.
#
# What it did not weigh is the OTHER reach, and this repository already uses
# it: `bin/check-solver-pin --network` fetches what kiosk.tech actually serves,
# because a commit in that repository can break a pin here without triggering
# this workflow. The direction is INVERTED for the onboarding page -- a commit
# HERE breaks a page THERE -- so this script belongs on this side, where the
# drift is authored, next to the derivation check whose boundary it extends.
#
# THE PAGE IS RESOLVED IN A STATED ORDER, AND THE ORDER IS PRINTED:
#
#   1. `--page PATH`                 -- an explicit file.
#   2. `$KIOSK_TECH_DIR/onboarding.html`
#   3. `../kiosk.tech/onboarding.html` -- the sibling checkout, which is what a
#      workspace session and the convergence loop have. Tree against tree: this
#      mode is never spuriously red.
#   4. `--network`                   -- https://kiosk.tech/onboarding.html, what
#      an operator is actually reading. 3 attempts with backoff, so a blip does
#      not read as drift.
#
# IF NONE OF THEM RESOLVES, THIS EXITS 1 AND SAYS THE CHECK DID NOT RUN. It
# does not skip. A guard that quietly no-ops when its subject is absent is
# indistinguishable from a guard that passed, and that is the failure this
# script exists to close rather than to commit.
#
# THE COST OF `--network`, stated so nobody is surprised by it: between a
# commit here that moves a verb and the sibling's deploy of the matching page,
# it is CORRECTLY red. That is why it is not on `push` -- it would gate every
# merge in this repository on a third-party fetch, which is the trade
# `check-solver-pin` already declined in writing -- and why the CI job runs it
# on the daily schedule, where a red default branch notifies, exactly as
# `solver-pin-live` does. The `--self-test` arm has no such dependency and DOES
# run on every push, because a rule that has stopped matching anything is the
# other way this goes quietly green.
#
# ── WHAT A RULE IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A rule DERIVES a fact from this repository and holds the page to it. Nothing
# below is ever compared to a second hand-typed copy of the same fact, because
# that moves the defect rather than closing it. Two kinds:
#
#   DERIVATION  the page must AGREE with a value read out of this repository.
#   BAN         the page must not contain a construct this repository refuses.
#               A ban's subject list is derived too, so it cannot rot into a
#               list of names nobody maintains.
#
# THE VACUITY ARM. Every rule declares what it needs to find in order to be
# holding anything at all -- a derivation that computes, a live site in the
# page, a non-empty derived subject list. A rule that finds none of that FAILS
# and names itself, rather than passing. That is not decoration: one repository
# over, a relink rule required a word that no live sentence used, so it matched
# nothing for its entire existence and nothing said so.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT CATCH, so nothing here is overtrusted:
#   * Prose. The page's sentences about the verb are not derived from anything.
#   * A verb the page teaches that has no counterpart here at all.
#   * The page agreeing with a getgrocery that is itself wrong. Consistency,
#     never truth.
#   * Whether the FLOOR is right. RAILS_FLOOR and RUBY_FLOOR hold the page to
#     what the gemspecs DECLARE. Whether the declaration is itself honest is a
#     different question, answered by the CI leg that runs the suites on the
#     declared Ruby floor -- not by this script.
#   * Whether the SERVED page equals the sibling's working tree. `--network`
#     answers about the served bytes and the default mode about the tree; they
#     are two questions and the run says which one it asked.
#
# Stdlib only: no bundle, no services, no database. It reads files, and reaches
# the network only under `--network`.
#
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation             # sibling checkout
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --network   # the published page
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --list      # every rule and what it derives
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --self-test # red on a plant of every rule

require "net/http"
require "uri"
require "set"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# The three files in THIS repository every rule below derives from.
GENERATOR_INITIALIZER = "kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/templates/initializer.rb.tt"
GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/controllers/kiosk/storefront_controller.rb"
GETGROCERY_ORDERS     = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/controllers/kiosk/orders_controller.rb"
GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/config/initializers/kiosk.rb"
# The engine's gemspec is the ONLY one that names Rails at all, so it is the
# single authority for the floor an adopting operator has to clear (K-1320).
SERVER_GEMSPEC = "kiosk-server/kiosk-server.gemspec"
# kiosk-core's `required_ruby_version` is the floor every gemspec declares, and
# is the same source `bin/check-demo-prereqs` derives its Ruby line from -- so
# the page and the eight demo READMEs cannot drift apart (K-1321).
CORE_GEMSPEC = "kiosk-core/kiosk-core.gemspec"
# The one place either published repository states publication status and the
# canonical install form; the page points at it and must agree with it (K-1319).
MONOREPO_README = "README.md"
# The rescue seam the page teaches under «raise what you would raise anyway»,
# and the file holding the sentences it renders INSTEAD of the exception's own
# (K-1341). Two files because the two halves of that fact live apart: the mixin
# decides WHETHER the message travels, `errors.rb` decides WHAT does.
SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN = "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/handler_mixin.rb"
SERVER_ERRORS        = "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/errors.rb"

PAGE_URL      = "https://kiosk.tech/onboarding.html"
PAGE_BASENAME = "onboarding.html"

# The verb both sides declare, and the one K-1325's drift was in.
WORKED_VERB = "delivery_slots"

# ── Reading the sources ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def repo_read(rel)
  path = File.join(ROOT, rel)
  raise "derivation source missing: #{rel}" unless File.file?(path)

  File.read(path)
end

# GET with retries. A transient blip must not read as drift, so "could not
# reach it" and "reached it and the bytes are wrong" stay different answers.
def fetch(url, attempts: 3)
  last = nil
  attempts.times do |i|
    sleep(2**i) if i.positive?
    begin
      res = Net::HTTP.get_response(URI(url))
      return [res.body, nil] if res.is_a?(Net::HTTPOK)

      last = "HTTP #{res.code}"
    rescue StandardError => e
      last = "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
    end
  end
  [nil, "unreachable after #{attempts} attempts (#{last})"]
end

# The page, plus a sentence saying WHERE it came from. Never a silent skip:
# every branch either returns bytes with a provenance or returns the reason
# there are none, and the caller exits 1 on the second.
def resolve_page(explicit:, network:)
  if explicit
    return [nil, nil, "--page #{explicit} does not exist"] unless File.file?(explicit)

    return [File.read(explicit), "--page #{explicit}", nil]
  end

  env_dir = ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"]
  if env_dir && !env_dir.empty?
    path = File.join(env_dir, PAGE_BASENAME)
    return [nil, nil, "KIOSK_TECH_DIR is set but #{path} does not exist"] unless File.file?(path)

    return [File.read(path), "KIOSK_TECH_DIR #{path}", nil]
  end

  sibling = File.expand_path("../kiosk.tech/#{PAGE_BASENAME}", ROOT)
  return [File.read(sibling), "sibling checkout #{sibling}", nil] if File.file?(sibling) && !network

  if network
    body, err = fetch(PAGE_URL)
    return [nil, nil, "#{PAGE_URL}: #{err}"] if body.nil?

    return [body, "the published page #{PAGE_URL} (#{body.bytesize} bytes)", nil]
  end

  [nil, nil,
   "no sibling checkout at #{sibling}, no KIOSK_TECH_DIR, no --network. " \
   "THIS CHECK DID NOT RUN -- it is not a pass."]
end

# ── Ruby-source parsing, shared by both sides ────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything below is a static read of declaration text. It never evaluates
# anything, and it is deliberately narrow: it looks only at the constructs both
# sides actually use, and a construct it does not recognise makes the rule that
# needed it VACUOUS (and therefore red) rather than absent.

# Strip Ruby comments and the contents of double-quoted strings, so a `{` in a
# description sentence cannot be mistaken for a hash brace. Bytes are replaced
# rather than removed, so every index still lines up with the original.
# Memoised: `masked` is a pure function of its input, and the self-test runs
# every rule once per arm — thirty-odd times over the same handful of sources.
# Without this the char-by-char walk below is re-run several hundred times and
# the arm suite takes minutes instead of seconds.
MASK_CACHE = {}

def masked(src)
  cached = MASK_CACHE[src]
  return cached if cached

  MASK_CACHE[src] = masked!(src)
end

def masked!(src)
  out    = src.dup
  i      = 0
  len    = out.length
  in_str = false
  while i < len
    c = out[i]
    if in_str
      if c == "\\"
        out[i] = " "
        out[i + 1] = " " if i + 1 < len && out[i + 1] != "\n"
        i += 2
        next
      end
      if c == '"'
        in_str = false
      elsif c != "\n"
        out[i] = " "
      end
    elsif c == '"'
      in_str = true
    elsif c == "#"
      # A comment runs to end of line. Blank it, newline kept so line numbers
      # and every later index still line up with the original.
      while i < len && out[i] != "\n"
        out[i] = " "
        i += 1
      end
      next
    end
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

# STRUCTURE IS FOUND ON THE MASKED COPY, VALUES ARE READ OFF THE ORIGINAL.
# `masked` preserves every byte position, so a range located in one indexes the
# other. Reading a value off the masked copy is how the first writing of this
# script derived `required: []` from a line that plainly says
# `required: ["delivery_address"]` -- the quoted names had been blanked, both
# sides came back empty, and the rule compared two nothings and passed.
#
# The declaration text a `def <name>` claims: from the `kind :` macro that
# opens it up to the `def` line itself, as a Range.
def declaration_range(mask, verb)
  at = mask.index(/^\s*def #{Regexp.escape(verb)}\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  kinds = mask[0...at].enum_for(:scan, /^\s*kind\s+:/).map { Regexp.last_match.begin(0) }
  return nil if kinds.empty?

  (kinds.last...at)
end

# The `input_schema ...` part of a declaration -- up to whatever macro comes
# next, so `output_schema`'s own `required:` can never be read as the input's.
def input_schema_range(mask, decl_range)
  region = mask[decl_range]
  from   = region.index(/^\s*input_schema\b/)
  return nil if from.nil?

  stop = region.index(/^\s*(?:output_schema|example_params|example_row|reach|def)\b/, from + 1)
  base = decl_range.first + from
  (base...(stop ? decl_range.first + stop : decl_range.last))
end

# Every `input_schema ...` region in a whole source, as Ranges.
def input_schema_ranges(mask)
  ranges = []
  mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*input_schema\b/).each do
    from = Regexp.last_match.begin(0)
    stop = mask.index(/^\s*(?:output_schema|example_params|example_row|reach|def)\b/, from + 1)
    ranges << (from...(stop || mask.length))
  end
  ranges
end

# `required: ["a", "b"]` or `required: %w[a b]`, as a list of names -- the one
# at the TOP LEVEL of the slice, never a nested one.
#
# THE DEPTH IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND THE FIRST WRITING OF THIS FUNCTION DID NOT
# HAVE IT. A plain "first `required:` in the region" reads
# `create_order`'s ITEM schema -- `required: ["sku", "qty"]`, nested two braces
# down inside `items:` -- and never reaches the argument list the verb actually
# requires. Both sides answered `["sku", "qty"]`, so a rule comparing them
# compared two copies of the same nested fact and passed on a page that
# required an argument getgrocery leaves optional. That is the identical shape
# this file's own header records about reading values off the masked copy: two
# nothings, equal, green.
#
# Structure is walked on the MASKED copy (a bracket inside a description
# sentence is not depth) and the literal is read off the ORIGINAL at the same
# index (the names live inside string literals, which the mask blanks).
def top_level_required(mask_slice, src_slice)
  depth = 0
  i     = 0
  while i < mask_slice.length
    c = mask_slice[i]
    if ["{", "["].include?(c)
      depth += 1
    elsif ["}", "]"].include?(c)
      depth -= 1
    elsif depth.zero? && mask_slice[i, 9] == "required:"
      m = src_slice[i..].match(/\Arequired:\s*(\[[^\]]*\]|%w\[[^\]]*\])/m)
      return m[1].scan(/[a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*/) if m
    end
    i += 1
  end
  nil
end

# The property names declared at the TOP level of a `properties: {...}` hash.
def property_names(slice)
  at = slice.index(/properties:\s*\{/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  open  = slice.index("{", at)
  depth = 0
  names = []
  i     = open
  while i < slice.length
    c = slice[i]
    if c == "{"
      depth += 1
    elsif c == "}"
      depth -= 1
      break if depth.zero?
    elsif depth == 1 && (m = slice[i..].match(/\A([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{/)) &&
          (i.zero? || slice[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/)
      names << m[1]
    end
    i += 1
  end
  names
end

# The top-level entries of a `properties: {...}` hash as {name => Range}, where
# the Range spans that property's own brace-balanced body. Structure is walked
# on the MASKED copy and the range indexes the ORIGINAL, which is the same
# discipline `verb_input_schema` uses: a `{` inside a description sentence is
# not structure, and a `type: "array"` inside a string literal is not readable
# from the mask.
def property_body_ranges(mask, base)
  slice = mask[base]
  at    = slice.index(/properties:\s*\{/)
  return {} if at.nil?

  open  = slice.index("{", at)
  depth = 0
  out   = {}
  i     = open
  while i < slice.length
    c = slice[i]
    if c == "{"
      depth += 1
    elsif c == "}"
      depth -= 1
      break if depth.zero?
    elsif depth == 1 && (m = slice[i..].match(/\A([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{/)) &&
          (i.zero? || slice[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/)
      bstart = i + m[0].length - 1
      d      = 0
      j      = bstart
      while j < slice.length
        d += 1 if slice[j] == "{"
        if slice[j] == "}"
          d -= 1
          break if d.zero?
        end
        j += 1
      end
      out[m[1]] = ((base.first + bstart)..(base.first + j))
    end
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

# Every INPUT parameter a source declares as an ARRAY OF OBJECTS -- the subject
# list of the element-shape ban, derived rather than typed. A declaration whose
# own body says both `type: "array"` and `type: "object"` is an array whose
# elements are objects, which is exactly the shape a handler may not index
# without checking (K-1339).
def array_of_object_param_names(src)
  names = Set.new
  mask  = masked(src)
  input_schema_ranges(mask).each do |range|
    property_body_ranges(mask, range).each do |name, body|
      text = src[body]
      names << name if text.include?('type: "array"') && text.include?('type: "object"')
    end
  end
  names
end

# Every VERB a source declares: the mixin's contract is that each class-level
# `kind :` macro is claimed by the NEXT `def`, so that pairing is the whole
# rule. A `def` that no macro reaches -- a private helper, a plain module
# function -- is not a verb and is not returned, which is why this cannot be
# replaced by "every def in the file".
def declared_verbs(src)
  mask  = masked(src)
  kinds = mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*kind\s+:/).map { Regexp.last_match.begin(0) }
  defs  = mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*def ([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)/)
              .map { [Regexp.last_match.begin(0), Regexp.last_match(1)] }
  kinds.filter_map { |k| defs.find { |(at, _)| at > k }&.last }.uniq
end

# The BODY a `def <verb>` opens, as a Range: from the def line to whatever ends
# the declaration -- the next `kind :` macro, or the `private` keyword that
# opens the helper section. Scoping a ban to one verb's body is what keeps
# "this argument is optional HERE" from being read against a verb that requires
# the same name.
def handler_body_range(mask, verb)
  at = mask.index(/^\s*def #{Regexp.escape(verb)}\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  stop = mask.index(/^\s*(?:kind\s+:|private\s*$)/, at + 1)
  (at...(stop || mask.length))
end

# Every parameter name declared `type: "integer"` in a source's INPUT schemas.
# The subject list of the coercion ban, derived rather than typed, so a rename
# here reddens the rule instead of emptying it. Input schemas only: an output
# `price_cents: { type: "integer" }` is a field the operator RENDERS, never an
# argument anything coerces, and the first writing of this rule swept four
# names in where two belong.
def integer_param_names(src)
  names = Set.new
  mask  = masked(src)
  input_schema_ranges(mask).each do |range|
    src[range].scan(/([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{[^{}]*type:\s*"integer"/m) { names << Regexp.last_match(1) }
  end
  names
end

# The page ships its Ruby inside `<pre><code class="language-ruby">` with five
# characters entity-escaped. Rules about CODE read this, not the raw HTML: a
# `-&gt;` lambda and an `&amp;&amp;` would otherwise parse as neither Ruby nor
# prose, and a sentence in the surrounding page would be searched as if it were
# a handler.
ENTITIES = { "&lt;" => "<", "&gt;" => ">", "&quot;" => '"', "&#39;" => "'", "&amp;" => "&" }.freeze

def page_ruby(page)
  page.scan(%r{<code class="language-ruby">(.*?)</code>}m)
      .flatten
      .map { |b| b.gsub(/&(?:lt|gt|quot|#39|amp);/) { |e| ENTITIES[e] } }
      .join("\n")
end

# The class names a `c.handlers = %w[...]` line registers, in order. Reads a
# commented example too, which is what the generator template carries.
def handler_names(src)
  src.scan(/c\.handlers\s*=\s*%w\[([^\]]*)\]/).map { |m| m[0].split }
end

# The Rails MAJOR.MINOR the engine's gemspec declares. Every Rails component it
# names must agree: a gemspec pinning railties at `~> 8.1` and activerecord at
# `~> 8.0` has no single floor to state, so this returns nil and the rule that
# needs it reports itself VACUOUS rather than picking one.
RAILS_COMPONENTS = %w[railties actionpack activerecord activesupport].freeze

def rails_floor(gemspec_src)
  found = RAILS_COMPONENTS.filter_map do |c|
    m = gemspec_src.match(/add_dependency\s+"#{c}"\s*,\s*"~>\s*(\d+\.\d+)"/)
    m && m[1]
  end
  return nil if found.empty?

  found.uniq.length == 1 ? found.first : nil
end

# The Ruby floor a gemspec declares, as it is written: `>= 3.2.0`.
def ruby_floor(gemspec_src)
  m = gemspec_src.match(/required_ruby_version\s*=\s*">=\s*(\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"/)
  m && m[1]
end

# The canonical install's SOURCE QUALIFIER, read off the meta-gem line in the
# monorepo README: ["github", "kiosk-hq/kiosk"]. When the gems are published and
# that line loses its qualifier this returns nil, and INSTALL_SOURCE reports
# itself vacuous -- which is the correct moment for a human to restate the rule,
# not a moment to pass quietly.
def canonical_install_source(readme_src)
  m = readme_src.match(/^gem\s+"kiosk-all"\s*,\s*(github|git|path|source):\s*"([^"]+)"/)
  m && [m[1], m[2]]
end

# Every Gemfile line in the page's Ruby that installs a Kiosk gem.
def page_gem_lines(page)
  page_ruby(page).lines.select { |l| l.match?(/^\s*gem\s+"kiosk-[a-z0-9-]+"/) }
end

# ── The rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each returns [ok, description, detail]. `detail` is printed either way, so a
# green run is a measurement and not only an exit code.

FAILURES = []

def check(name, ok, what, detail)
  status = ok ? "ok  " : "FAIL"
  puts format("  %s %-22s %s", status, name, what)
  puts "         #{detail}" unless detail.to_s.empty?
  FAILURES << "#{name}: #{what} -- #{detail}" unless ok
  ok
end

def vacuous!(name, why)
  puts format("  %s %-22s VACUOUS -- holds nothing", "FAIL", name)
  puts "         #{why}"
  FAILURES << "#{name}: VACUOUS -- #{why}"
  false
end

# R1 -- the page's handler class names are the generator's worked example.
def rule_handler_names(page, sources)
  derived = handler_names(sources[:generator]).first
  return vacuous!("HANDLER_NAMES", "no `c.handlers = %w[...]` in #{GENERATOR_INITIALIZER}") if derived.nil? || derived.empty?

  found = handler_names(page_ruby(page))
  return vacuous!("HANDLER_NAMES", "the page declares no `c.handlers = %w[...]` line") if found.empty?

  bad = found.reject { |list| list == derived }
  check("HANDLER_NAMES",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{found.size} c.handlers line(s) name #{derived.join(" ")}",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GENERATOR_INITIALIZER}" : "page names #{bad.map { |b| b.join(" ") }.uniq.join(" | ")}")
end

# The input_schema of one verb, in one source: [required list, property names].
def verb_input_schema(src, verb)
  mask  = masked(src)
  decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
  return nil if decl.nil?

  range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
  return nil if range.nil?

  # `required:` at the TOP LEVEL of the input_schema, located on the masked copy
  # and read off the ORIGINAL (its names live inside string literals); a nested
  # `required:` -- an item schema's, say -- is a different fact and is not this
  # one. `properties:` off the MASKED copy (its names are identifiers, and a
  # brace inside a description sentence must not be counted as structure).
  [top_level_required(mask[range], src[range]), property_names(mask[range])]
end

# R2 -- the worked verb's REQUIRED list is getgrocery's.
def rule_slots_required(page, sources)
  demo = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema in #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT}") if demo.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "no input_schema `required:` on getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB}") if demo[0].nil? || demo[0].empty?

  mine = verb_input_schema(page_ruby(page), WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "the page declares no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema") if mine.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "the page's #{WORKED_VERB} declares no input_schema `required:`") if mine[0].nil?

  check("SLOTS_REQUIRED",
        mine[0].sort == demo[0].sort,
        "#{WORKED_VERB} required: #{demo[0].sort.inspect}",
        "page says #{mine[0].sort.inspect} (derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT})")
end

# R3 -- and the same verb declares the same PARAMETERS.
def rule_slots_properties(page, sources)
  demo = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema in #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT}") if demo.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "no input_schema `properties:` on getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB}") if demo[1].nil? || demo[1].empty?

  mine = verb_input_schema(page_ruby(page), WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "the page declares no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema") if mine.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "the page's #{WORKED_VERB} declares no input_schema `properties:`") if mine[1].nil?

  check("SLOTS_PROPERTIES",
        mine[1].sort == demo[1].sort,
        "#{WORKED_VERB} parameters: #{demo[1].sort.inspect}",
        "page declares #{mine[1].sort.inspect} (derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT})")
end

# R4 -- BAN. No declared-integer argument is read with `.to_i`.
#
# Over the page's Ruby with COMMENTS MASKED, deliberately: the page explains
# why `.to_i` is not the spelling it uses, and quoting the idiom in order to
# refuse it is the opposite of teaching it. What is banned is the executable
# line, which is the only thing an operator copies.
def rule_integer_coercion(page, sources)
  names = integer_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | integer_param_names(sources[:orders])
  return vacuous!("INTEGER_COERCION", "getgrocery declares no `type: \"integer\"` input parameter") if names.empty?

  code = masked(page_ruby(page))
  live = names.select { |n| code.include?(n) }
  if live.empty?
    return vacuous!("INTEGER_COERCION",
                    "none of getgrocery's integer parameters (#{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")}) is read in the page's Ruby")
  end

  pattern = /\[:(?:#{live.map { |n| Regexp.escape(n) }.join("|")})\]\s*(?:&\.|\.)to_i\b/
  hits    = code.scan(pattern)
  check("INTEGER_COERCION",
        hits.empty?,
        "no `.to_i` on #{live.sort.join(", ")} (#{live.size} live of #{names.size} derived)",
        hits.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : "#{hits.size} site(s): #{hits.uniq.join(", ")}")
end

# R5 -- BAN. No frozen calendar literal, anywhere on the page.
def rule_frozen_date(page, _sources)
  unless page.include?("example_params(")
    return vacuous!("FROZEN_DATE", "the page declares no `example_params(`, so there is nothing this ban protects")
  end

  hits = page.scan(/(?<![0-9a-fA-F-])20[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}/)
  check("FROZEN_DATE",
        hits.empty?,
        "no YYYY-MM-DD literal on the page",
        hits.empty? ? "a literal ages into the 400 the verb answers a past date with" : "#{hits.size} literal(s): #{hits.uniq.join(", ")}")
end

# R6 -- the page must NAME getgrocery's real handler classes wherever they
# differ from the ones it teaches, so it cannot present one as the other.
def rule_getgrocery_handlers(page, sources)
  demo = handler_names(sources[:getgrocery_init]).first
  return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "no `c.handlers = %w[...]` in #{GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER}") if demo.nil? || demo.empty?

  mine = handler_names(page_ruby(page)).first
  return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "the page declares no `c.handlers = %w[...]` line") if mine.nil? || mine.empty?

  if demo.sort == mine.sort
    return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS",
                    "getgrocery now registers exactly the classes the page teaches (#{demo.join(" ")}); " \
                    "this rule held the DIFFERENCE and now holds nothing -- restate it or delete it")
  end

  divergent = demo - mine
  missing   = divergent.reject { |c| page.include?(c) }
  check("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS",
        missing.empty?,
        "the page names getgrocery's own #{divergent.join(", ")}",
        missing.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER}" : "not named on the page: #{missing.join(", ")}")
end

# The (required, properties) pair getgrocery declares for a verb, wherever the
# verb lives -- the read surface or the write surface. One lookup, so a rule
# reaches every verb the two controllers declare instead of one named constant.
def demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
  verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], verb) || verb_input_schema(sources[:orders], verb)
end

# Every verb BOTH getgrocery and the page declare. The subject list for the
# verb-by-verb rules below, derived on both sides: a verb renamed in either
# place leaves the set rather than silently keeping a stale comparison alive.
def shared_verbs(page, sources)
  demo = declared_verbs(sources[:storefront]) | declared_verbs(sources[:orders])
  demo & declared_verbs(page_ruby(page))
end

# R7 -- BAN. The page must not make required, in the HANDLER, an argument
# getgrocery's own declaration leaves optional. This is the half of K-1325 that
# lived in code rather than in the schema.
#
# EVERY SHARED VERB, EACH SCOPED TO ITS OWN HANDLER BODY (K-1338). It used to
# derive from one named verb and scan the whole page, which could not have
# reached the write verbs -- and must not be widened by unioning the optional
# sets either: `delivery_address` is optional on `reschedule_delivery` and
# REQUIRED on `create_order`, so a page-wide ban on requiring it would be red
# for doing the right thing one verb over.
def rule_no_require_optional(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  mask     = masked(page_ruby(page))
  subjects = 0
  hits     = []
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
    next if demo.nil?

    req, props = demo
    next if req.nil? || props.nil?

    optional = props - req
    next if optional.empty?

    body = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if body.nil?

    subjects += optional.size
    pattern = /params\.require\(:(?:#{optional.map { |n| Regexp.escape(n) }.join("|")})\)/
    mask[body].scan(pattern) { hits << "#{verb}: #{Regexp.last_match(0)}" }
  end

  if subjects.zero?
    return vacuous!("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",
                    "no verb of #{shared.join(", ")} leaves a declared parameter optional in getgrocery, " \
                    "so this ban has no subject")
  end

  check("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",
        hits.empty?,
        "no `params.require` on the #{subjects} optional argument(s) of #{shared.sort.join(", ")}",
        hits.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : hits.join(" | "))
end

# R11 -- every verb both sides declare REQUIRES what getgrocery requires, and
# nothing more (K-1338).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page required `delivery_date` on BOTH write verbs
# where getgrocery requires it on neither -- omitting it books tomorrow, for the
# same reason `delivery_slots` leaves its own `date` optional (the caller cannot
# compute the operator's today). An operator copying the page shipped a stricter
# contract than the origin it was copied from, and the schema half of K-1325 one
# verb over had no rule reaching it.
#
# TWO DIRECTIONS, because one of them alone is half a comparison:
#   OVER   the page requires a name getgrocery does not -- the K-1338 shape.
#   UNDER  the page DECLARES a parameter getgrocery requires and leaves it
#          optional -- a cart an operator's own origin would refuse.
# A parameter the page does not declare at all is neither: the page teaches a
# deliberately smaller surface (it does not show `create_order`'s replace-an-
# unpaid-order `order_id`), and that is a simplification, not drift.
def rule_write_required(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("WRITE_REQUIRED", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  ruby     = page_ruby(page)
  compared = []
  derived  = 0
  bad      = []
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
    mine = verb_input_schema(ruby, verb)
    next if demo.nil? || mine.nil?

    dreq, = demo
    mreq, mprops = mine
    next if dreq.nil? || mreq.nil? || mprops.nil?

    compared << verb
    derived  += dreq.size
    over  = mreq - dreq
    under = (dreq & mprops) - mreq
    bad << "#{verb} requires #{over.sort.inspect}, which getgrocery leaves optional" if over.any?
    bad << "#{verb} leaves #{under.sort.inspect} optional, which getgrocery requires" if under.any?
  end

  if compared.empty? || derived.zero?
    return vacuous!("WRITE_REQUIRED",
                    "none of #{shared.join(", ")} declares a non-empty input_schema `required:` on " \
                    "getgrocery's side, so there is nothing to hold the page to")
  end

  check("WRITE_REQUIRED",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{compared.size} shared verb(s) (#{compared.sort.join(", ")}) require exactly what getgrocery requires " \
        "(#{derived} name(s) derived)",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : bad.join(" | "))
end

# R12 -- BAN. A handler that indexes the elements of a declared ARRAY OF
# OBJECTS must check the element IS one first (K-1339).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page's `create_order` ran `item[:sku]` over every
# cart entry with no shape check, so `items: ["bread"]` -- a well-formed request
# with the wrong shape, which an assistant does send -- raised inside the
# handler and reached the caller as a 500 about the operator's server.
# getgrocery answers a typed 400 naming the shape it wanted. The page is what an
# operator copies, so the page taught the 500.
#
# The subject list is DERIVED: every input parameter getgrocery declares whose
# own body says both `type: "array"` and `type: "object"`. A site is a page verb
# whose handler body reads `params[:<that name>]`; the guard the site must carry
# is `is_a?(Hash)`, read off the MASKED body so a comment explaining the idiom
# cannot stand in for running it.
def rule_item_shape(page, sources)
  names = array_of_object_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | array_of_object_param_names(sources[:orders])
  return vacuous!("ITEM_SHAPE", "getgrocery declares no array-of-objects input parameter") if names.empty?

  ruby  = page_ruby(page)
  mask  = masked(ruby)
  sites = []
  declared_verbs(ruby).each do |verb|
    body = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if body.nil?

    text = mask[body]
    names.each do |name|
      next unless text.include?("params[:#{name}]")

      sites << [verb, name, text.include?("is_a?(Hash)")]
    end
  end

  if sites.empty?
    return vacuous!("ITEM_SHAPE",
                    "no page handler reads any of getgrocery's array-of-objects parameters " \
                    "(#{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")})")
  end

  missing = sites.reject { |(_v, _n, ok)| ok }
  check("ITEM_SHAPE",
        missing.empty?,
        "#{sites.size} site(s) reading #{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")} guard the element with `is_a?(Hash)`",
        missing.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : "unguarded: #{missing.map { |(v, n, _)| "#{v}/#{n}" }.join(", ")}")
end

# R8 -- the page states the Rails floor the engine's gemspec declares.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (K-1320): `kiosk-server` requires Rails `~> 8.1`, which
# excludes 7.x and 8.0.x -- most of the "existing Rails apps" this page is
# addressed to -- and the page, whose whole framing is "assuming a working Rails
# + Postgres app you already know", never mentioned the one constraint most
# likely to disqualify the reader's app. Bundler's refusal is the only place it
# was stated, at the end of `bundle install`.
def rule_rails_floor(page, sources)
  floor = rails_floor(sources[:server_gemspec])
  if floor.nil?
    return vacuous!("RAILS_FLOOR",
                    "#{SERVER_GEMSPEC} declares no single `~> MAJOR.MINOR` shared by " \
                    "#{RAILS_COMPONENTS.join(", ")}")
  end
  return vacuous!("RAILS_FLOOR", "the page does not mention Rails at all") unless page.include?("Rails")

  hits = page.scan("Rails #{floor}").length
  check("RAILS_FLOOR",
        hits.positive?,
        "the page names Rails #{floor} (#{hits} occurrence(s))",
        hits.positive? ? "derived from #{SERVER_GEMSPEC}" : "the page names Rails but never `Rails #{floor}`")
end

# R9 -- and the Ruby floor every gemspec declares.
def rule_ruby_floor(page, sources)
  floor = ruby_floor(sources[:core_gemspec])
  return vacuous!("RUBY_FLOOR", "#{CORE_GEMSPEC} declares no `required_ruby_version = \">= X.Y.Z\"`") if floor.nil?
  return vacuous!("RUBY_FLOOR", "the page does not mention Ruby at all") unless page.include?("Ruby")

  hits = page.scan("Ruby #{floor}").length
  check("RUBY_FLOOR",
        hits.positive?,
        "the page names Ruby #{floor} (#{hits} occurrence(s))",
        hits.positive? ? "derived from #{CORE_GEMSPEC}" : "the page names Ruby but never `Ruby #{floor}`")
end

# R10 -- every Kiosk gem the page tells an operator to add carries the SOURCE
# QUALIFIER the monorepo README declares canonical.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (K-1319): four different install instructions lived
# across the two published repositories, two of them resolving against RubyGems,
# where no Kiosk gem is published -- and nothing said so in either direction.
# Publication status and the canonical form are now stated ONCE, in the
# monorepo README; this holds the page to that one statement instead of to a
# second copy of it.
def rule_install_source(page, sources)
  canonical = canonical_install_source(sources[:readme])
  if canonical.nil?
    return vacuous!("INSTALL_SOURCE",
                    "#{MONOREPO_README} declares no canonical `gem \"kiosk-all\", <source>: \"...\"` " \
                    "line -- if the gems have been published and the qualifier is gone, restate " \
                    "this rule rather than leaving it matching nothing")
  end

  key, value = canonical
  lines = page_gem_lines(page)
  return vacuous!("INSTALL_SOURCE", "the page's Ruby carries no `gem \"kiosk-...\"` line") if lines.empty?

  qualifier = "#{key}: \"#{value}\""
  bad = lines.reject { |l| l.include?(qualifier) }
  check("INSTALL_SOURCE",
        bad.empty?,
        "all #{lines.length} `gem \"kiosk-*\"` line(s) carry `#{qualifier}`",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{MONOREPO_README}" : "#{bad.length} without it: #{bad.map(&:strip).join(" | ")}")
end

# ── K-1341 / K-1344 derivations ─────────────────────────────────────────────

# The rescue seam's body: `def kiosk_rescue_to_wire` down to its own `end`, by
# indentation. Read off the MASKED copy for structure and returned from the
# ORIGINAL, the same discipline every reader above uses.
def rescue_seam_body(src)
  mask = masked(src)
  # `[ \t]*` and NOT `\s*`: `masked` blanks a comment to SPACES, so the
  # forty-line header above this def is whitespace in the mask and a `\s*`
  # that crosses newlines matches at the top of it -- which makes `indent` a
  # multi-line blob and the closing `end` unfindable. Measured: the first
  # writing of this reader answered VACUOUS on a seam that is plainly there.
  at   = mask.index(/^[ \t]*def kiosk_rescue_to_wire\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  indent = mask[at..].match(/\A([ \t]*)def/)[1]
  stop   = mask.index(/^#{indent}end$/, at + 1)
  return nil if stop.nil?

  src[at..stop]
end

# The class the seam re-raises UNTOUCHED — `raise exception if
# exception.is_a?(X)`. That class is the operator's deliberate route to the
# assistant, so the page must name it; deriving it means a rename in the engine
# reddens the page rather than leaving a stale class name published.
def reraised_class(seam)
  return nil if seam.nil?

  m = seam.match(/raise\s+\w+\s+if\s+\w+\.is_a\?\(([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_:]*)\)/)
  m && m[1]
end

# Every sentence `Errors.rescued_wire` gives the CALLER in place of the
# exception's own, as {code => hint}. Ruby's adjacent-string continuation is
# joined, because that is how the constant is written and a half-sentence would
# never match a page.
def rescued_hints(src)
  body = src[/RESCUED_HINTS\s*=\s*\{(.*?)\}\.freeze/m, 1]
  return {} if body.nil?

  out = {}
  body.scan(/"([a-z_]+)"\s*=>\s*((?:"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"\s*\\?\s*)+),/m) do |code, literal|
    out[code] = literal.scan(/"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"/).flatten.join
  end
  out
end

# R13 -- the page teaches «raise what you would raise anyway»; the engine no
# longer publishes what that raise SAID, and the page must carry both halves
# of that (K-1341, closing K-1310's blast radius one repository over).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: after K-1310 the rescue seam renders
# {Errors.rescued_wire}'s sentence and hint for the code it decided, and sends
# the exception's class, message and backtrace to the operator's log instead of
# the wire. The page's paragraph stayed ACCURATE — it only ever claimed the
# STATUS maps to a code — and stopped being COMPLETE: an operator reading it
# learned the raise-and-map recovery and not that the sentence they raised with
# is dropped, nor that rendering the envelope or raising a Kiosk error class is
# how they speak to the assistant on purpose. `kiosk-server/README.md` gained
# exactly that split in the same wave and the published page did not.
#
# TWO DERIVED HALVES, because either alone is half the instruction:
#   WHAT ARRIVES INSTEAD  one of {Errors::RESCUED_HINTS}' sentences, verbatim.
#   THE DELIBERATE ROUTE  the class the seam re-raises untouched, by name.
#
# AND A THIRD STATE THAT IS NEITHER: if the seam ever renders the exception's
# own message again, this rule's subject is gone and the page's paragraph
# becomes FALSE rather than merely incomplete. That is reported as VACUOUS —
# red, naming itself — so the page is re-decided rather than quietly held to a
# fact that stopped being one.
def rule_error_redaction(page, sources)
  seam = rescue_seam_body(sources[:handler_mixin])
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "no `def kiosk_rescue_to_wire` in #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}") if seam.nil?

  if seam.match?(/\w+\.message/)
    return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION",
                    "#{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s rescue seam renders an exception's own `.message` again, so " \
                    "there is no redaction for the page to teach -- the page's paragraph is now FALSE and " \
                    "this rule must be re-decided, not deleted")
  end

  klass = reraised_class(seam)
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "#{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s seam re-raises no class untouched") if klass.nil?

  hints = rescued_hints(sources[:server_errors])
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "no RESCUED_HINTS map in #{SERVER_ERRORS}") if hints.empty?

  # The page may quote any ONE of them -- it teaches one worked refusal, not
  # the whole table -- but it must quote one exactly, or it is paraphrasing a
  # sentence this engine sends.
  quoted = hints.select { |_code, hint| page.include?(hint) }
  # `Errors::Base` is the ancestor the seam tests; an operator raises a
  # SUBCLASS, so the page is held to the namespace and either spelling passes.
  needle = klass.sub(/::Base\z/, "")
  named  = page.include?(needle)

  bad = []
  bad << "the page quotes none of the #{hints.size} `RESCUED_HINTS` sentences the wire sends in place of " \
         "the exception's own" if quoted.empty?
  bad << "the page never names #{needle}, the class #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s seam re-raises untouched -- " \
         "so it does not say how an operator speaks to the assistant deliberately" unless named

  check("ERROR_REDACTION",
        bad.empty?,
        "the page carries the redaction split: #{quoted.keys.sort.join(", ")} hint(s) quoted verbatim, " \
        "#{needle} named",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN} + #{SERVER_ERRORS}" : bad.join(" | "))
end

# The JSON Schema assertion keywords a property declaration can carry. This
# list is the STANDARD's vocabulary, not a fact about this repository -- what
# is derived is which of them getgrocery actually uses, on which property, with
# what value, and a keyword nobody declares contributes nothing.
ASSERTION_KEYWORDS = %w[format pattern enum const minimum maximum exclusiveMinimum
                        exclusiveMaximum multipleOf minLength maxLength minItems
                        maxItems uniqueItems].freeze

# A property's OWN assertion keywords, as {keyword => value-as-written}.
# DEPTH 1 ONLY: `items:`'s body contains the element schema, whose `qty` has a
# `maximum` of its own, and reading that as the array's would compare two facts
# that are not the same fact -- the identical shape `top_level_required` exists
# for one macro up.
KEYWORD_AT = /\G([a-zA-Z]+):[ \t]*/

def property_assertions(src, base)
  mask  = masked(src)
  out   = {}
  property_body_ranges(mask, base).each do |name, body|
    pairs = {}
    depth = 0
    i     = body.first
    while i <= body.last
      c = mask[i]
      if ["{", "["].include?(c)
        depth += 1
      elsif ["}", "]"].include?(c)
        depth -= 1
        break if depth.zero? && i > body.first
      elsif depth == 1 && (i.zero? || mask[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/) &&
            # `\G` anchors the match AT `i` without slicing the string: the
            # first writing sliced `mask[i..body.last]` on every character,
            # which is quadratic and made the self-test take minutes.
            (m = KEYWORD_AT.match(mask, i)) && ASSERTION_KEYWORDS.include?(m[1])
        pairs[m[1]] = assertion_value(src[(i + m[0].length)..body.last])
      end
      i += 1
    end
    out[name] = pairs
  end
  out
end

# The value as WRITTEN, so `maximum: 6` and `maximum: WireArguments::MAX_INT4`
# stay distinguishable. A bracketed value is read to its matching `]` (an
# `enum: ["a", "b"]` has a comma inside it); anything else runs to the comma or
# the end of the line.
def assertion_value(text)
  return "" if text.nil?

  if text.start_with?("[")
    depth = 0
    i     = 0
    while i < text.length
      depth += 1 if text[i] == "["
      if text[i] == "]"
        depth -= 1
        break if depth.zero?
      end
      i += 1
    end
    text[0..i]
  else
    text[/\A[^,\n]*/].to_s.strip
  end
end

# A value this page could have written for itself. A Ruby constant
# (`UuidCheck::JSON_SCHEMA_PATTERN`) is not: the page teaches a standalone
# controller and has no such constant to name, so its absence there is a
# simplification rather than drift, and the rule counts it instead of
# asserting on it.
def literal_assertion?(value)
  value.match?(/\A("|\d|-|\[|true\b|false\b)/)
end

def demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
  [sources[:storefront], sources[:orders]].each do |src|
    mask  = masked(src)
    decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
    next if decl.nil?

    range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
    next if range.nil?

    return property_assertions(src, range)
  end
  nil
end

def page_property_assertions(ruby, verb)
  mask  = masked(ruby)
  decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
  return nil if decl.nil?

  range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
  return nil if range.nil?

  property_assertions(ruby, range)
end

# R14 -- every property both sides declare carries the same VALIDATION
# KEYWORDS, so the page cannot publish a shape the origin does not perform
# (K-1344).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page declared `format: "date"` on `delivery_date`
# for both write verbs where getgrocery declares no `format` there at all --
# deliberately, because that argument is parsed with `Date.parse` and a bare
# "20260101" is published behaviour on that verb pair (K-1324). MEASURED with
# json_schemer 2.5.0, which asserts `format` by default: the page's schema
# refuses "20260101" with a wire 400 before the handler runs, and the handler
# the page shows beside it is written to accept it. So an operator copying the
# page shipped a schema stricter than its own handler and than the reference.
# Eleven rules preceded this one and not one compared a property's KEYWORDS.
#
# TWO DIRECTIONS, and they are not symmetrical:
#   OVER   the page declares a keyword (or a different value) getgrocery does
#          not -- the K-1344 shape, and the one that REFUSES real input.
#   UNDER  getgrocery declares a keyword whose value is a JSON literal and the
#          page omits it -- a copied schema looser than the origin.
# UNDER is scoped to literals ON PURPOSE: getgrocery writes
# `pattern: UuidCheck::JSON_SCHEMA_PATTERN`, a constant belonging to a class the
# page's standalone controller does not have, so requiring it there would be red
# for a difference that is not drift. Those are COUNTED AND PRINTED on every
# run rather than asserted -- an honest number beats a rule someone switches off.
def rule_property_keywords(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  ruby      = page_ruby(page)
  pairs     = []            # [verb, prop, demo_assertions, page_assertions]
  derived   = 0
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
    mine = page_property_assertions(ruby, verb)
    next if demo.nil? || mine.nil?

    (demo.keys & mine.keys).sort.each do |prop|
      derived += demo[prop].size
      pairs << [verb, prop, demo[prop], mine[prop]]
    end
  end

  if pairs.empty? || derived.zero?
    return vacuous!("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",
                    "no property shared by #{shared.sort.join(", ")} carries a JSON Schema assertion " \
                    "keyword on getgrocery's side, so there is nothing to hold the page to")
  end

  bad        = []
  uncomparable = []
  pairs.each do |verb, prop, demo, mine|
    (mine.keys - demo.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} declares #{kw}: #{mine[kw]}, which getgrocery does not declare at all"
    end
    (mine.keys & demo.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      next if mine[kw] == demo[kw]

      if literal_assertion?(demo[kw]) && literal_assertion?(mine[kw])
        bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} declares #{kw}: #{mine[kw]} where getgrocery declares #{demo[kw]}"
      else
        uncomparable << "#{verb}.#{prop}.#{kw} (getgrocery writes #{demo[kw]})"
      end
    end
    (demo.keys - mine.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      if literal_assertion?(demo[kw])
        bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} omits #{kw}: #{demo[kw]}, which getgrocery declares"
      else
        uncomparable << "#{verb}.#{prop}.#{kw} (getgrocery writes #{demo[kw]})"
      end
    end
  end

  detail = bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : bad.join(" | ")
  unless uncomparable.empty?
    detail += "; #{uncomparable.size} keyword(s) NOT compared, getgrocery's value is a Ruby constant " \
              "the page cannot name: #{uncomparable.join(", ")}"
  end
  check("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{pairs.size} shared propert(ies) across #{shared.size} verb(s) carry getgrocery's keywords " \
        "(#{derived} keyword(s) derived)",
        detail)
end

RULES = [
  [:rule_handler_names,      "HANDLER_NAMES",       "derivation", "the two handler class names the kiosk:install initializer template teaches"],
  [:rule_slots_required,     "SLOTS_REQUIRED",      "derivation", "getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} input_schema `required:` list"],
  [:rule_slots_properties,   "SLOTS_PROPERTIES",    "derivation", "getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} input_schema property names"],
  [:rule_integer_coercion,   "INTEGER_COERCION",    "ban",        "every parameter getgrocery declares `type: \"integer\"`"],
  [:rule_frozen_date,        "FROZEN_DATE",         "ban",        "a YYYY-MM-DD literal, which the verb refuses once it is past"],
  [:rule_getgrocery_handlers, "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "derivation", "the handler classes getgrocery's own initializer registers"],
  [:rule_no_require_optional, "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",    "ban",        "params.require on an argument getgrocery leaves optional"],
  [:rule_rails_floor,        "RAILS_FLOOR",         "derivation", "the Rails MAJOR.MINOR kiosk-server's gemspec declares"],
  [:rule_ruby_floor,         "RUBY_FLOOR",          "derivation", "the Ruby floor kiosk-core's gemspec declares"],
  [:rule_install_source,     "INSTALL_SOURCE",      "derivation", "the install source qualifier the monorepo README declares canonical"],
  [:rule_write_required,     "WRITE_REQUIRED",      "derivation", "the input_schema `required:` list of every verb both sides declare"],
  [:rule_item_shape,         "ITEM_SHAPE",          "ban",        "indexing an element of a declared array-of-objects without checking it is a Hash"],
  [:rule_error_redaction,    "ERROR_REDACTION",     "derivation", "what the rescue seam sends INSTEAD of the exception's own message, and the class it re-raises untouched"],
  [:rule_property_keywords,  "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",   "derivation", "the JSON Schema assertion keywords getgrocery declares on every property both sides declare"],
].freeze

def load_sources
  { generator:       repo_read(GENERATOR_INITIALIZER),
    storefront:      repo_read(GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT),
    orders:          repo_read(GETGROCERY_ORDERS),
    getgrocery_init: repo_read(GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER),
    server_gemspec:  repo_read(SERVER_GEMSPEC),
    core_gemspec:    repo_read(CORE_GEMSPEC),
    readme:          repo_read(MONOREPO_README),
    handler_mixin:   repo_read(SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN),
    server_errors:   repo_read(SERVER_ERRORS) }
end

def run_rules(page, sources)
  RULES.each { |m, _n, _k, _d| send(m, page, sources) }
end

# ── --list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def run_list
  puts "#{RULES.size} rules, each deriving from this repository:"
  RULES.each do |_m, name, kind, what|
    puts format("  %-22s %-11s %s", name, kind, what)
  end
  puts
  puts "Derivation sources:"
  [GENERATOR_INITIALIZER, GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT, GETGROCERY_ORDERS, GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER,
   SERVER_GEMSPEC, CORE_GEMSPEC, MONOREPO_README, SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN, SERVER_ERRORS].each do |rel|
    puts "  #{File.exist?(File.join(ROOT, rel)) ? "present" : "MISSING"}  #{rel}"
  end
  0
end

# ── --self-test ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each arm plants ONE faithful break -- the shape a ledger row actually
# recorded, never a token nobody would type -- and asserts the rule goes red on
# it. Nothing on disk is touched: every plant is a string transformation of an
# in-memory copy. A VACUITY arm per rule blanks the DERIVATION side instead, so
# a rule that has stopped being able to compute its own subject fails rather
# than passing.
#
# IT RUNS WITH NO SIBLING AND NO NETWORK, which is the whole reason it can be
# the push-blocking half of the CI job. When no page resolves it plants into a
# FIXTURE built from these same derivations -- so the fixture cannot rot, and
# the run says in its first line which of the two it used. The derivation side
# is always the real checkout, in both cases.
#
# THE PLANTS ARE DERIVED, not typed. A plant table naming `date` and
# `delivery_address` would be a hand-kept copy of the very facts these rules
# exist to stop being hand-kept: rename a parameter in getgrocery and the arm
# would quietly stop matching, go green, and read as coverage.

def self_test_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)
  FAILURES.clear
  quiet { run_rules(page, sources) }
  hit = FAILURES.any? { |f| f.start_with?("#{expect_rule}:") }
  puts format("  %s  %-48s %s", hit ? "red " : "GREEN", label, hit ? "-> #{expect_rule}" : "!! #{expect_rule} stayed green")
  hit
end

def quiet
  old = $stdout
  $stdout = File.open(File::NULL, "w")
  yield
ensure
  $stdout.close
  $stdout = old
end

# Every value the plants and the fixture are built from, read out of the repo.
def derived_facts(sources)
  handlers = handler_names(sources[:generator]).first
  req, props = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  ints = (integer_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | integer_param_names(sources[:orders])).to_a.sort
  { handlers: handlers,
    required: req,
    properties: props,
    optional: props - req,
    integers: ints,
    # The write-surface facts. `orders_schemas` is every verb getgrocery's
    # orders controller declares, with its top-level (required, properties)
    # pair; `arrays` is every input parameter declared as an array of objects.
    # Both are derived, so a rename in getgrocery reddens an arm rather than
    # quietly emptying it.
    orders_schemas: declared_verbs(sources[:orders]).filter_map { |v|
      sch = verb_input_schema(sources[:orders], v)
      [v, sch] if sch && !sch[0].nil? && !sch[1].nil?
    }.to_h,
    arrays: (array_of_object_param_names(sources[:storefront]) |
             array_of_object_param_names(sources[:orders])).to_a.sort,
    getgrocery: handler_names(sources[:getgrocery_init]).first,
    # K-1341: the rescue seam itself, and the sentences it sends in place of
    # the exception's own. Both derived, so a reworded hint or a renamed class
    # reddens the arm rather than leaving it matching a string nobody sends.
    seam:  rescue_seam_body(sources[:handler_mixin]),
    hints: rescued_hints(sources[:server_errors]),
    rails: rails_floor(sources[:server_gemspec]),
    ruby: ruby_floor(sources[:core_gemspec]),
    install: canonical_install_source(sources[:readme]) }
end

# A minimal page that satisfies every rule by construction, so the arms
# below are testing the RULES rather than the fixture.
def fixture_page(f)
  ruby_init = "Kiosk.configure do |c|\n  c.handlers = %w[#{f[:handlers].join(" ")}]\nend\n"
  props     = f[:properties].map { |n| "                 #{n}: { type: \"string\" }," }.join("\n")
  reads     = f[:integers].map { |n| "    #{n} = whole_number(params[:#{n}])" }.join("\n")
  ruby_verb = <<~RUBY
    class #{f[:handlers].first} &lt; ApplicationController
      include Kiosk::Handler

      kind :query
      input_schema type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
                   properties: {
    #{props}
                   },
                   required: [#{f[:required].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]
      example_params({})
      def #{WORKED_VERB}
    #{reads}
        Date.parse(params[:#{f[:optional].first}].to_s)
      end
    end
  RUBY
  key, value = f[:install]
  gemfile = %(# Gemfile\ngem "kiosk-all", #{key}: "#{value}"\n)
  [%(<p>getgrocery registers #{(f[:getgrocery] - f[:handlers]).join(", ")} instead.</p>),
   %(<p>Needs Rails #{f[:rails]} and Ruby #{f[:ruby]}.</p>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{gemfile}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{ruby_init}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{ruby_verb}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{fixture_write_surface(f)}</code></pre>)].join("\n")
end

# The write half of the fixture: one declaration per verb getgrocery's orders
# controller declares, carrying that verb's own required list and parameters.
# WRITE_REQUIRED and ITEM_SHAPE have nothing to hold without it -- and a fixture
# that made them VACUOUS would fail the unplanted arm rather than pass it, which
# is the point of the vacuity tier.
def fixture_write_surface(f)
  blocks = f[:orders_schemas].map do |verb, (req, props)|
    prop_lines = props.map { |n| "                 #{n}: { type: \"string\" }," }.join("\n")
    # A verb that declares an array-of-objects parameter reads it the way the
    # ban requires: element shape checked before anything indexes it.
    reads = (props & f[:arrays]).map { |n|
      "    return if params[:#{n}].is_a?(Array) && params[:#{n}].all? { |item| item.is_a?(Hash) }"
    }.join("\n")
    <<~VERB
      kind :action
        input_schema type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
                     properties: {
      #{prop_lines}
                     },
                     required: [#{req.map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]
        example_params({})
        def #{verb}
      #{reads}
        end
    VERB
  end
  "class #{f[:handlers].last} &lt; ApplicationController\n  include Kiosk::Handler\n\n  " +
    blocks.join("\n  ") + "end\n"
end

def run_self_test
  sources = load_sources
  f       = derived_facts(sources)
  if f[:handlers].nil? || f[:required].nil? || f[:properties].nil? || f[:optional].empty? ||
     f[:integers].empty? || f[:rails].nil? || f[:ruby].nil? || f[:install].nil? ||
     f[:orders_schemas].empty? || f[:arrays].empty? || f[:seam].nil? || f[:hints].empty?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its own plants from this checkout: #{f.inspect}"
    return 1
  end

  page, provenance, = resolve_page(explicit: nil, network: false)
  real = !page.nil?
  page ||= fixture_page(f)
  puts "page side: #{real ? provenance : "BUILT-IN FIXTURE (no page resolved; derivation side is still this checkout)"}"
  puts

  int      = f[:integers].include?("delivery_slot_id") ? "delivery_slot_id" : f[:integers].first
  opt      = f[:optional].first
  req_lit  = "required: [#{f[:required].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]"
  divergent = (f[:getgrocery] - f[:handlers]).first

  # The WRITE_REQUIRED plant needs a required name that belongs to exactly ONE
  # verb, so the substitution below cannot land on another verb's list. Derived
  # by counting names across every required list this checkout declares --
  # never typed, because a typed "items" would go on matching a verb that had
  # been renamed and the arm would pass while covering nothing.
  all_required = f[:orders_schemas].values.map(&:first) + [f[:required]]
  write_anchor = f[:orders_schemas].values.map(&:first).flatten
                                   .find { |n| all_required.count { |l| l.include?(n) } == 1 }
  array_param  = f[:arrays].first

  # K-1341's two halves, both read off the live engine.
  errors_ns      = reraised_class(f[:seam]).to_s.sub(/::Base\z/, "")
  redaction_hint = f[:hints].values.find { |h| page.include?(h) } || f[:hints].values.first

  # K-1344's plant coordinates, derived from the live pair rather than typed:
  #   KW_OVER   a property BOTH sides declare on which getgrocery asserts
  #             nothing -- the only place a page-side keyword is unambiguously
  #             one getgrocery does not have.
  #   KW_VALUE  a keyword and literal value getgrocery uses SOMEWHERE, so the
  #             plant is a real keyword of this schema family and not a token.
  #   KW_UNDER  a literal-valued keyword both sides carry, for the direction a
  #             one-sided rule would miss.
  kw_over = kw_under = kw_value = nil
  ruby_now = page_ruby(page)
  shared_verbs(page, sources).each do |verb|
    d = demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
    m = page_property_assertions(ruby_now, verb)
    next if d.nil? || m.nil?

    d.each_value { |as| as.each { |k, v| kw_value ||= [k, v] if literal_assertion?(v) } }
    (d.keys & m.keys).sort.each do |prop|
      kw_over ||= [verb, prop] if d[prop].empty? && m[prop].empty?
      d[prop].each do |kw, v|
        kw_under ||= [verb, prop, kw, v] if literal_assertion?(v) && m[prop][kw] == v
      end
    end
  end
  if errors_ns.empty? || redaction_hint.nil? || kw_over.nil? || kw_under.nil? || kw_value.nil?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its K-1341/K-1344 plants: " \
         "errors_ns=#{errors_ns.inspect} hint=#{redaction_hint.inspect} " \
         "over=#{kw_over.inspect} under=#{kw_under.inspect} value=#{kw_value.inspect}"
    return 1
  end

  puts "── planted breaks (page side) ──"
  arms = []
  arms << self_test_arm("a handler class the generator does not teach",
                        page.sub(f[:handlers].first, "Kiosk::PlantedController"), sources, "HANDLER_NAMES")
  arms << self_test_arm("#{WORKED_VERB} requires #{opt.inspect} instead",
                        page.sub(req_lit, "required: [#{opt.inspect}]"), sources, "SLOTS_REQUIRED")
  arms << self_test_arm("the #{opt.inspect} parameter renamed away",
                        page.sub(/\b#{Regexp.escape(opt)}:(\s*)\{(\s*)type:/, "#{opt}_planted:\\1{\\2type:"), sources, "SLOTS_PROPERTIES")
  arms << self_test_arm("#{int} read with .to_i again",
                        page.sub("whole_number(params[:#{int}])", "params[:#{int}].to_i"), sources, "INTEGER_COERCION")
  arms << self_test_arm("a calendar literal frozen into example_params",
                        page.sub("example_params({", 'example_params({ planted: "2026-08-12",'), sources, "FROZEN_DATE")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery's own #{divergent} un-named",
                        page.gsub(divergent, "the read controller"), sources, "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS")
  arms << self_test_arm("params.require(:#{opt}) back in the handler",
                        page.sub("params[:#{opt}]", "params.require(:#{opt})"), sources, "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL")
  # gsub, not sub: the page names each floor more than once, and a plant that
  # leaves one copy standing proves nothing about the rule.
  arms << self_test_arm("the page names a Rails line the gemspec excludes",
                        page.gsub("Rails #{f[:rails]}", "Rails 7.1"), sources, "RAILS_FLOOR")
  arms << self_test_arm("the page names a Ruby below the declared floor",
                        page.gsub("Ruby #{f[:ruby]}", "Ruby 2.7.0"), sources, "RUBY_FLOOR")
  # sub, not gsub: ONE gem line loses the qualifier while its siblings keep it.
  # That is the half-relink shape -- the one a wave actually ships -- and a gsub
  # plant would never exercise it.
  arms << self_test_arm("one gem line loses the canonical source qualifier",
                        page.sub(%(#{f[:install][0]}: "#{f[:install][1]}"), ""), sources, "INSTALL_SOURCE")
  # K-1338's shape: ONE verb's required list gains a name getgrocery does not
  # require. Renaming the anchor inside the list produces both halves at once —
  # a name required here and nowhere in getgrocery, and a name getgrocery
  # requires that the page now declares but no longer requires.
  arms << self_test_arm("#{write_anchor.inspect} required under another name",
                        page.sub(/(required:\s*\[[^\]]*)"#{Regexp.escape(write_anchor)}"/) {
                          "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}\"#{write_anchor}_planted\""
                        }, sources, "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  # And the other direction, the one a "page requires too much" rule alone
  # would miss: an argument getgrocery REQUIRES, still declared on the page,
  # demoted to optional.
  arms << self_test_arm("#{write_anchor.inspect} demoted to optional on the page",
                        page.sub(/(required:\s*\[[^\]]*)"#{Regexp.escape(write_anchor)}",\s*/) {
                          Regexp.last_match(1)
                        }, sources, "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  # K-1339's shape: the element-shape guard removed from the handler that
  # indexes a declared array of objects.
  arms << self_test_arm("the #{array_param} element-shape guard removed",
                        page.gsub("is_a?(Hash)", "respond_to?(:[])"), sources, "ITEM_SHAPE")
  # K-1341's shape, both halves: the page paraphrases the sentence the wire
  # actually sends, and the page stops naming the deliberate route.
  arms << self_test_arm("the quoted #{errors_ns.split("::").last} hint paraphrased",
                        page.sub(redaction_hint, "check your arguments and try again."),
                        sources, "ERROR_REDACTION")
  arms << self_test_arm("the page stops naming #{errors_ns}",
                        page.gsub(errors_ns, "a Kiosk error"), sources, "ERROR_REDACTION")
  # K-1344's shape: a property BOTH sides declare gains a keyword getgrocery
  # does not, so the page publishes a stricter shape than the origin performs.
  arms << self_test_arm("#{kw_over[0]}.#{kw_over[1]} gains #{kw_value[0]}: #{kw_value[1]}",
                        page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(kw_over[1])}:\s*\{\s*type:\s*"[a-z]+")/,
                                 "\\1, #{kw_value[0]}: #{kw_value[1]}"),
                        sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")
  # And the other direction, which a "the page is too strict" rule alone would
  # not reach: a literal keyword getgrocery declares, dropped from the page.
  arms << self_test_arm("#{kw_under[0]}.#{kw_under[1]} loses #{kw_under[2]}: #{kw_under[3]}",
                        page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(kw_under[1])}:\s*\{[^}]*?),\s*#{kw_under[2]}:\s*#{Regexp.escape(kw_under[3])}/) {
                          Regexp.last_match(1)
                        }, sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")

  puts "── vacuity (derivation side blanked) ──"
  arms << self_test_arm("generator template loses its c.handlers example", page,
                        sources.merge(generator: sources[:generator].gsub(/c\.handlers\s*=\s*%w\[[^\]]*\]/, "c.handlers = []")), "HANDLER_NAMES")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} loses required:", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].sub(req_lit, "")), "SLOTS_REQUIRED")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} is renamed away", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].sub(/^(\s*)def #{WORKED_VERB}$/, "\\1def #{WORKED_VERB}_renamed")), "SLOTS_PROPERTIES")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery declares no integer input parameter", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].gsub('type: "integer"', 'type: "number"'),
                                      orders: sources[:orders].gsub('type: "integer"', 'type: "number"')), "INTEGER_COERCION")
  arms << self_test_arm("the page loses every example_params",
                        page.gsub("example_params(", "example_args("), sources, "FROZEN_DATE")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery registers exactly the page's classes", page,
                        sources.merge(getgrocery_init: sources[:getgrocery_init].sub(divergent, f[:handlers].first)), "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS")
  # REQUIRE_OPTIONAL's subject is «an argument some SHARED verb leaves
  # optional», so blanking one verb no longer empties it -- the ban reaches
  # every verb both sides declare. This makes EVERY verb of both controllers
  # require every parameter it declares, verb by verb, off its own derived
  # (required, properties) pair.
  require_everything = lambda do |src|
    declared_verbs(src).reduce(src) do |acc, verb|
      sch = verb_input_schema(src, verb)
      next acc if sch.nil? || sch[0].nil? || sch[1].nil? || sch[1].empty?

      acc.sub("required: [#{sch[0].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]",
              "required: [#{sch[1].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]")
    end
  end
  arms << self_test_arm("every getgrocery verb requires every parameter it declares", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: require_everything.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: require_everything.call(sources[:orders])), "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL")
  arms << self_test_arm("the engine's gemspec stops naming Rails", page,
                        sources.merge(server_gemspec: sources[:server_gemspec].gsub(/add_dependency\s+"(?:#{RAILS_COMPONENTS.join("|")})"[^\n]*/, "# removed")), "RAILS_FLOOR")
  arms << self_test_arm("kiosk-core stops declaring required_ruby_version", page,
                        sources.merge(core_gemspec: sources[:core_gemspec].gsub(/required_ruby_version[^\n]*/, "# removed")), "RUBY_FLOOR")
  arms << self_test_arm("the README's canonical line loses its source qualifier", page,
                        sources.merge(readme: sources[:readme].gsub(/^gem\s+"kiosk-all"[^\n]*/, %(gem "kiosk-all"))), "INSTALL_SOURCE")
  # Both write-surface rules derive their SUBJECT from getgrocery, so blanking
  # that side must make them say so rather than pass. A renamed verb on the demo
  # side leaves nothing shared to compare.
  renamed = ->(src) { src.gsub(/^(\s*)def ([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)$/) { "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}def #{Regexp.last_match(2)}_renamed" } }
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery declares no verb the page also declares", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: renamed.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: renamed.call(sources[:orders])), "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery declares no array-of-objects parameter", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].gsub('type: "array"', 'type: "string"'),
                                      orders: sources[:orders].gsub('type: "array"', 'type: "string"')), "ITEM_SHAPE")
  arms << self_test_arm("the engine's RESCUED_HINTS map is emptied", page,
                        sources.merge(server_errors: sources[:server_errors]
                          .sub(/RESCUED_HINTS\s*=\s*\{.*?\}\.freeze/m, "RESCUED_HINTS = {}.freeze")),
                        "ERROR_REDACTION")
  # Renaming the keywords rather than deleting them keeps the declarations
  # parseable, so the rule reaches its own vacuity test instead of failing
  # earlier for an unrelated reason.
  dekeyword = ->(src) { src.gsub(/\b(#{ASSERTION_KEYWORDS.join("|")}):/) { "x#{Regexp.last_match(1)}:" } }
  arms << self_test_arm("getgrocery asserts no JSON Schema keyword anywhere", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: dekeyword.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: dekeyword.call(sources[:orders])), "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")

  FAILURES.clear
  puts
  puts "── unplanted ──"
  run_rules(page, sources)
  clean = FAILURES.empty?
  puts "  #{clean ? "ok" : "FAIL"}   #{real ? "the working tree" : "the built-in fixture"} holds all #{RULES.size} rules"

  bad = arms.count(false)
  puts
  # Name the flag in the output. A run that never says it self-tested is no
  # evidence the flag did anything, because a script that IGNORES an
  # unrecognised flag and performs its ordinary run looks identical from
  # outside -- so the line below is the only thing distinguishing the two.
  if bad.zero? && clean
    puts "check-onboarding-derivation --self-test: OK — #{arms.size} arm(s) " \
         "(#{arms.size - RULES.size} planted breaks, #{RULES.size} vacuity), every one caught."
  else
    puts "check-onboarding-derivation --self-test: FAIL — #{arms.size} arm(s), " \
         "#{bad} did not go red; unplanted run #{clean ? "clean" : "RED"}."
  end
  bad.zero? && clean ? 0 : 1
end

# ── main ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

network  = ARGV.delete("--network")
list     = ARGV.delete("--list")
selftest = ARGV.delete("--self-test")
explicit = nil
if (i = ARGV.index("--page"))
  explicit = ARGV[i + 1]
  ARGV.slice!(i, 2)
end
unless ARGV.empty?
  warn "unknown argument(s): #{ARGV.join(" ")}"
  exit 2
end

exit run_list if list
exit run_self_test if selftest

page, provenance, reason = resolve_page(explicit: explicit, network: !network.nil?)
if page.nil?
  warn "check-onboarding-derivation: #{reason}"
  exit 1
end

puts "onboarding.html read from #{provenance}"
puts "derivations read from this checkout (#{ROOT})"
puts
sources = load_sources
run_rules(page, sources)
puts
if FAILURES.empty?
  puts "#{RULES.size} rules, all held."
  exit 0
end
puts "#{FAILURES.size} of #{RULES.size} rules FAILED:"
FAILURES.each { |f| puts "  - #{f}" }
exit 1
